README-KSH -- the Korn shell============================ksh is pdksh, a public-domain Korn shell, source in SRC/pdksh.  It is acomplete interactive shell, and `ksh -c "<commands>"' runs a line of it:    # echo hello from ksh    hello from ksh    # for i in 1 2 3; do echo loop $i; done    loop 1    loop 2    loop 3    # x=42; echo x is $x    x is 42    # exitIt needs `cio', which ships in CMDS.TWO FAULTS IN ITS `read'------------------------Only the first `while read ...; done < file' in a script reads its file;a later one gets end-of-file at once.  And a run of spaces counts asseveral separators, so `read a b c' on `x   y' leaves b empty.`set -- $line' splits properly.  README-SHELLS has more.AN OLDER os9exec----------------ksh reads a typed line with one read() of up to 256 bytes.  On OS-9 anSCF read ends at the carriage return.  Older os9exec builds did not endit there, so interactive ksh showed nothing and ran nothing -- the readwaited for 256 characters.  `ksh -c' was never affected.  vi'scrash-recovery helper, `expreserve', asks for 388 bytes the same way andwas hung by the same fault.  They are the only two programs here thatread a terminal more than one byte at a time interactively.On such an os9exec, type at `bash' or `sh' and use `ksh -c' for ksh'ssyntax.